{"kind":"request","text":"Thirteenth-round review of the deployment note at exact head fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f, artifact 29044602fdd4b2e3564ccf554a97e78764c94cac. One subject.\n\nEverything I added this round is new syntax or a new rule, and all of it was written by me in response to your findings, so none of it has had adversarial contact. Treat the header line as the highest-risk item: base, profile, entries and root=true are four new fields introduced in one edit, and my record on new mechanisms in repair rounds is five for five.\n\nSpecifically. Is parenting the profile blob actually sufficient, or have I moved the retention problem rather than solved it — does an auditor need anything beyond that blob to execute the rules, an interpreter, a schema, other referenced files, and if so I have made the same mistake at one remove. Does entries=\u003cn\u003e add anything a parent count does not already give, or is it a redundant field that can disagree with the topology and create a fault where none exists. Does base=\u003cP\u003e need to be checkable against something, given a lying drain could write any P into its own header — I think the sequence itself constrains it for admitted batches but I am not sure it does for an all-refused one, which is exactly the case that motivated the field.\n\nThe deduplication rule needs its edges probed. I specify collapsing by object id and listing every naming ref, but I have not thought hard about a submitter who aliases a candidate *another* submitter authored, or about whether refs= is attacker-controlled data being recorded verbatim into a durable record. Both feel like the kind of thing that looks fine until someone tries it.\n\nOn root=true: I claim the marker rather than the arity is the termination condition, and that a forged entry cannot become a second root by dropping a parent. That is asserted. The ledger ref is protected and append-only, so a second root should be unreachable, but I have not measured what a walker does if it encounters one, and \"cannot happen\" arguments are how I got the last three wrong.\n\nReport APPROVED or CHANGES-REQUESTED against this exact head with verbatim full event ids.","body":{"conditions":"Review notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md at exact head fbed0df7020b61f275eff622557c457cc7933d0f. Judge closed or open: the header line carrying base and content-addressed profile with the profile blob parented; deduplication of aliased candidates with refs= listing; positional parent roles with root=true termination; case 4's absent-inbox assertion and three-run alias assertions with canonical-only positive control. Then attack the new material, none of which has had adversarial contact: whether parenting the profile blob is sufficient for an auditor to execute the rules or merely relocates the retention problem (interpreter, schema, referenced files); whether entries=\u003cn\u003e is redundant against the parent count and can disagree with the topology; whether base=\u003cP\u003e is checkable against anything for an all-refused batch where no admitted event constrains it; the deduplication edges including a submitter aliasing another submitter's candidate and whether refs= records attacker-controlled data verbatim; and whether the root=true termination claim and the cannot-have-two-roots argument hold under probe rather than assertion. Re-audit all spike cases for the cannot-fail class and for repaired-rule-with-surviving-old-sentence. Report APPROVED or CHANGES-REQUESTED against this exact head with verbatim full event ids.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}